INT. FUNERAL PARLOR SPEAKEASY - 1927 - NIGHT

Velvet drapes, casket stands doubling as bars. A jazz trio plays muted.

MAGDA, thirties, a bartender, pours gin into teacups. FOSTER, forties, a bootlegger, counts bills. LENNY, twenties, his nephew, dozes in a coffin cushion. ODETTE, twenties, a singer, sips at the piano. THE PIANIST, sixties, plays on.

A MAN, fifties, in a black frock coat and a funeral director's pin, steps in from the foyer.

MAGDA
Fosse's here.

She sets down the bottle. He surveys the room. Foster pockets his cash.

DIRECTOR
Evening. I run this establishment.

FOSTER
We've been paying your rent.

DIRECTOR
I'm told that was not rent. I am Mr. Whitcomb.

WHITCOMB
And I'd like my casket stands back.

Lenny wakes. He rolls out of the cushion. Odette stops singing. The pianist plays one wrong note and stops.
